"""Topic-specific teaching records. Primary-authored; no worker delegation."""
S=[]
def add(id,definition,terms,mechanism,case,fault,repair,qa,ref,kind='change'):
 S.append(dict(id=id,definition=definition,terms=[x.split('~') for x in terms.split('|')],mechanism=mechanism.split('|'),case=case,fault=fault,repair=repair,questions=[x.split('~') for x in qa.split('|')],refs=ref.split(),kind=kind))

add('s9-g0-t0','Python executes statements using objects, names and control flow; an automation program turns validated input into an explicit result or a reported failure.',
'Name~Binding to an object~Assignment does not copy a mutable object|Exception~Reported failure in execution~Catching everything can hide a failed change|Module~Importable namespace~Import-time side effects make tests hazardous',
'Parse the inventory before selecting any target.|Validate required values and compute a proposed result in a function.|Return structured evidence; catch expected errors at the command boundary.',
'A three-device inventory must produce three validation records before any deployment.','A broad exception handler prints success after a missing hostname.','Reject the malformed record with its index and exit unsuccessfully; valid records still produce deterministic output.',
'Why separate parsing from deployment?~Invalid input must fail before side effects.~A pure validation step is easier to inspect and repeat.|Does b = a copy a list?~No; both names reference the same list.~Mutating through either name affects the shared object.|Why use a main guard?~Keep CLI execution out of imports.~Tests can import functions without starting a change.|What should an unexpected exception do?~Propagate or produce a clear failure with context.~Do not mislabel incomplete execution as success.', 'python')
add('s9-g0-t1','Network automation combines inventory, protocol adapters and verification; Python is the coordinator, while device behavior remains platform-specific.',
'Inventory~Identified targets and intent~Reachability is not ownership|Adapter~Platform-specific operation boundary~Text commands are not a portable schema|Postcondition~Required observable result~Command delivery alone is insufficient',
'Resolve approved stable identities and normalize addresses.|Collect current state with bounded timeouts and authenticate the remote peer.|Compare intended and observed state after a small canary change.',
'Two lab routers have distinct management addresses and the same intended NTP server.','A loop uses a display name as an address and silently skips an unreachable target.','Validate addresses with ipaddress, retain per-target failures and withhold overall success until both postconditions are checked.',
'Why normalize addresses?~To compare actual address values instead of textual formatting.~IPv6 has multiple equivalent textual forms.|What does an SSH session prove?~Transport authentication and connection, not the requested configuration.~A post-read must test intent.|How should partial success be represented?~One outcome per target plus an overall incomplete result.~A single success string loses the failed device.|Why limit concurrency?~To bound device load and the size of a mistaken change.~Fast execution can amplify an inventory error.', 'python ip ssh')
add('s9-g0-t2','Data types constrain what values mean and which operations are valid; infrastructure schemas must distinguish text, numbers, booleans, sequences and keyed records.',
'String~Text value~A numeric-looking string is still text|Boolean~True or false value~Nonempty text false is truthy in Python|Dictionary~Keyed mapping~Missing and null are different states',
'Read raw data without assuming the decoder validated the business schema.|Check exact types and allowed ranges for each field.|Normalize once and pass the typed result to later steps.',
'Inventory requires enabled as a boolean and vlan as an integer from 1 through 4094.','The string false enables a branch because it is nonempty.','Reject string booleans and reject bool values where an integer VLAN is required; preserve false as an explicit value.',
'Why is bool checked separately from int?~Python booleans are instances of int.~A naive integer test accepts True as VLAN 1.|Can a set replace a device list?~Only when uniqueness matters and ordering is irrelevant.~Deployment order needs an explicit sequence.|What differs between missing and null?~Missing omits the field; null supplies an empty value.~Defaulting rules must define both cases.|Why avoid floats for VLANs?~Identifiers require exact integers in a bounded range.~Arithmetic convenience must not weaken validation.', 'python')
add('s9-g0-t3','Loops repeat a bounded operation over a collection; safe automation also defines stop conditions, per-item outcomes and retry limits.',
'Iteration~One pass over an item~An item failure needs its own record|Retry budget~Maximum repeated attempts~Retries can repeat side effects|Termination~Condition that ends work~An unbounded loop can overload a target',
'Freeze the approved target sequence and attempt budget.|Record each attempt and distinguish retryable transport faults from invalid intent.|Stop on exhausted budget or unsafe state and report unprocessed targets.',
'Three devices may each receive at most two read attempts.','A while loop retries an authentication rejection indefinitely.','Classify credential rejection as terminal, cap attempts and report the failed identity without its secret.',
'How many maximum calls for three targets and two attempts?~Six.~Count attempts rather than retries to avoid an off-by-one.|Should every error be retried?~No; invalid input and authorization rejection need correction.~Waiting does not repair policy.|Why avoid removing list items during iteration?~It changes positions and may skip later entries.~Build a separate result list.|What does break affect?~The innermost enclosing loop.~Outer target processing needs an explicit policy.', 'python','budget')
add('s9-g0-t4','A function gives a named operation explicit inputs and outputs; separating pure planning from side effects makes infrastructure changes testable.',
'Parameter~Input named by a function~Mutable defaults can survive across calls|Return value~Explicit function result~Printing is not a data contract|Side effect~Change outside the function result~Network writes need separate control',
'Define the input schema and failure behavior.|Compute a plan without changing the source objects.|Pass the reviewed plan to a separate executor and return evidence.',
'A planner compares current VLAN 10 with desired VLAN 20 and returns a delta.','A default list accumulates changes from earlier calls.','Use a fresh list per call and verify two independent calls return the same single change.',
'Why avoid a default list argument?~It is created once and can retain mutations.~Use None and create a new list inside.|What makes a planner deterministic?~Equivalent validated inputs yield equivalent plans.~Hidden clocks or network calls break this property.|Why return a record rather than print only?~Callers and tests can inspect structured fields.~Presentation belongs at the boundary.|Must a pure function mutate its input?~No.~Preserving the baseline allows reliable comparison.', 'python')
add('s9-g0-t5','File handling converts persistent bytes into application data; encoding, ownership and replacement behavior are part of the automation contract.',
'Encoding~Mapping between text and bytes~Platform defaults can corrupt non-ASCII text|Context manager~Guaranteed resource cleanup scope~Closing a file does not undo writes|Atomic replacement~Single-name replacement step~Filesystem and durability guarantees differ',
'Open an explicitly owned file with a declared encoding.|Validate the complete content before staging a replacement.|Replace only the owned destination, then read back and retain rollback bytes.',
'An owned UTF-8 intent file contains a site name with non-ASCII characters.','A partial write leaves invalid JSON after an interruption.','Stage and validate a sibling file, replace the owned path and test exact restoration; document that crash durability is not proven.',
'Why specify UTF-8?~So the byte contract is independent of the host default.~Correct display on one machine is not portability.|Does with roll back changed bytes?~No; it closes the resource.~Rollback requires retained content or another recovery mechanism.|Why validate before replacement?~To avoid installing malformed intent.~A successful write can contain unusable data.|What does a hash prove?~Byte identity relative to the compared digest.~It does not prove semantic correctness or backup independence.', 'python')
add('s9-g0-t6','JSON represents objects, arrays and scalar values; decoding valid JSON is only the first stage of validating infrastructure intent.',
'Object~String-keyed mapping~Duplicate keys need an explicit rejection policy|Array~Ordered values~Ordering may or may not be semantically meaningful|Schema~Allowed shape and values~Syntax alone does not enforce it',
'Decode JSON with a defined duplicate-key policy.|Reject unknown fields, wrong types and out-of-range values.|Serialize normalized data predictably for review and compare semantic state.',
'A JSON device record declares hostname edge-1 and VLAN 20.','Two vlan keys hide conflicting intent under a last-value-wins decoder.','Reject duplicate keys before planning; round-trip the normalized record and verify unchanged semantics.',
'Is valid JSON necessarily valid inventory?~No; the required schema may still be violated.~A list of strings can be valid JSON but wrong for a device record.|How does JSON null map to Python?~None.~It is not the string null.|Why sort keys for evidence?~To make equivalent mapping output easier to compare.~Array order still needs a contract.|What is unsafe about permissive duplicate keys?~One consumer may use a different value than another.~Reject ambiguity at the boundary.', 'python')
add('s9-g0-t7','YAML is a serialization language with mappings, sequences and scalars; parser version and schema determine how unquoted scalars are interpreted.',
'Mapping~Key-value collection~Indentation changes structure|Scalar~Single value~Implicit typing varies across schemas|Safe loader~Restricted construction policy~Safe loading is not business validation',
'Choose a supported parser and declare its YAML schema behavior.|Load without arbitrary object construction and inspect resulting types.|Validate inventory fields and reject ambiguous or duplicate definitions.',
'A playbook inventory contains a text site code and a boolean enabled field.','A parser coerces an unquoted identifier into a boolean or number.','Quote textual identifiers, use actual booleans for flags and test the parsed types with the deployed parser.',
'Why quote textual identifiers?~To preserve their intended string meaning.~Do not rely on all YAML versions sharing implicit typing.|Does indentation matter?~Yes; it determines nesting.~A visually nearby variable may belong to another host.|Does safe loading validate VLAN ranges?~No.~Construction safety and schema validity are separate.|Should secrets be plain YAML in Git?~No; store references or use an appropriate secret workflow.~Serialization provides no secrecy.', 'yaml ansible')
add('s9-g0-t8','Regular expressions match textual patterns; they are useful for constrained extraction but do not replace semantic validation or structured data models.',
'Anchor~Constraint on match position~Partial matches may accept extra text|Capture group~Extracted subexpression~Group success does not validate meaning|Semantic parser~Validator of a domain value~An IP-shaped string may be invalid',
'Define the expected whole line or bounded field.|Match with a small reviewed expression and reject unexpected structure.|Validate captured values with a domain parser before using them.',
'A parser extracts an IPv4-looking field from a synthetic interface line.','The pattern accepts 999.1.1.1 because it only checks digit groups.','Use regex for extraction and ipaddress for validity; test missing, extra and malformed fields.',
'Why use fullmatch for an entire identifier?~It requires the entire string to match.~Search can accept an unwanted surrounding payload.|Does a dot match a literal dot by default?~No; escape it for that meaning.~Metacharacters alter the accepted language.|Why bound input length?~To limit resource use and pathological matching.~Untrusted huge input is not a normal fixture.|When prefer structured output?~When the device provides a supported schema.~Human CLI layout can change across releases.', 'python ip')
add('s9-g1-t0','An API is a contract for requests, responses and failures between components; automation depends on its version, identity model and state semantics.',
'Contract~Inputs outputs and errors~Undocumented behavior is not a guarantee|Pagination~Partial collection retrieval~One page may omit targets|Rate limit~Service request budget~Concurrency must respect it',
'Discover the supported endpoint contract and authentication scope.|Read all required pages with bounded requests and schema checks.|Apply a reviewed operation and reconcile the returned state with the resource.',
'An inventory endpoint returns two pages of approved devices.','Only the first page is processed and the run claims full coverage.','Follow the documented next-page token, deduplicate stable IDs and report total coverage and any incomplete page.',
'Why use stable IDs?~Names may change or collide.~Updates must identify the intended resource.|What does a 2xx response establish?~The meaning defined by that status and endpoint.~Accepted work may still be asynchronous.|How handle unknown fields?~Follow a versioned schema policy.~Silently treating them as instructions is unsafe.|Why record a request ID?~To correlate a client action with server evidence.~It is more useful than logging a secret token.', 'http')
add('s9-g1-t1','REST-style APIs expose resources through representations and HTTP semantics; resource state and transport success must be interpreted separately.',
'Resource~Identified service object~URL shape alone does not prove REST constraints|Representation~Transferred view of state~May be partial or stale|Validator~Version marker such as ETag~Preconditions prevent lost updates',
'Read the resource representation and its version marker.|Compute a minimal intended change against that observed version.|Send the documented conditional operation and re-read after success.',
'Two operators edit the same interface description.','The second writer overwrites a change based on a stale representation.','Use a supported If-Match precondition; on conflict re-read and review a new plan rather than blindly retry.',
'Why distinguish resource from representation?~A response may expose only part of the resource.~Replacing it blindly can erase unobserved fields.|What does 412 indicate for If-Match?~The supplied precondition failed.~Refresh state before proposing a new write.|Is every JSON-over-HTTP endpoint RESTful?~No.~Encoding is independent of architectural constraints.|Why re-read after a write?~To confirm the intended observable state.~A transport response may not capture eventual convergence.', 'http')
add('s9-g1-t2','HTTP methods have defined semantics for reading, replacing and processing resource representations; retry safety depends on both those semantics and the endpoint contract.',
'Safe~Intended read-only semantics~Incidental logging can still occur|Idempotent~Repeated intended effect is equivalent~Responses can differ|Conditional request~Operation guarded by a validator~Support must be confirmed',
'Select the method prescribed by the endpoint.|Distinguish a received failure from a lost response with unknown outcome.|Retry only under an appropriate idempotency or reconciliation strategy.',
'A resource-creation POST times out after the server may have accepted it.','Blind retry creates a duplicate device record.','Reconcile by stable identity or use a documented idempotency key before resubmission.',
'Are GET and HEAD safe?~Yes under HTTP semantics.~Do not design state-changing actions behind them.|Is PUT idempotent?~Its intended effect is idempotent.~The second response need not be identical.|Is PATCH universally idempotent?~No; it depends on the patch operation.~Incrementing a value differs from assigning one.|Why is timeout not proof of failure?~The server may have acted before the response was lost.~Observe state before another non-idempotent request.', 'http')
add('s9-g1-t3','Authentication establishes an identity; authorization controls that identity’s actions, while transport security protects the channel and authenticates its peer.',
'Credential~Proof used to establish identity~Never place it in evidence logs|Scope~Permitted operation set~A valid token can still lack permission|Peer validation~Checking the remote identity~Encryption alone is insufficient',
'Retrieve a short-lived least-privilege credential through the approved secret path.|Validate server identity and send credentials only to the intended endpoint.|Handle expiry and authorization denial distinctly and redact diagnostic records.',
'A read-only service identity requests inventory over verified TLS.','A troubleshooting change disables certificate verification and logs the bearer token.','Restore trust verification, revoke the exposed token and retain only sanitized status and correlation data.',
'Does authentication imply write access?~No; authorization is separate.~Avoid widening scope to conceal a denied operation.|How differ 401 and 403?~401 concerns authentication requirements; 403 means the server refuses the request.~The endpoint may intentionally hide details.|Why rotate after exposure?~Deleting a log does not invalidate a copied credential.~Revoke the old capability.|What should a negative test demonstrate?~The identity cannot perform the excluded write.~A successful read alone cannot verify least privilege.', 'http ssh')
add('s9-g1-t4','Cisco programmability spans product-specific APIs and model-driven interfaces; IOS XE, NX-OS and controllers must be treated as distinct supported contracts.',
'Platform~Product and software release~A brand name is not an API schema|Capability~Advertised supported feature~Presence must be verified on the target|Model revision~Specific data contract~Paths can vary across revisions',
'Record product, release and supported API family.|Discover capabilities and model revisions before selecting a request.|Test one read and one denied operation, then validate any proposed change in an owned lab.',
'An IOS XE lab target is intended to expose model-driven interface data.','A request copied from a controller API returns an unknown resource.','Consult the exact IOS XE release guide and advertised model; fix the contract rather than weakening authentication.',
'Can an APIC path be assumed on IOS XE?~No.~Product APIs are not interchangeable.|Why record software release?~Feature and model availability vary.~A sample from another release is only a hypothesis.|Does enabling an API authorize all users?~No.~Service exposure and access control are separate.|What evidence precedes a write?~Capabilities, target identity, baseline and a supported rollback path.~A working GET is only one prerequisite.', 'cisco netconf restconf')
add('s9-g1-t5','NETCONF uses structured RPCs and capability-advertised datastore operations to manage network configuration; transactional features are optional capabilities.',
'RPC~Structured remote procedure call~Inspect rpc-error rather than transport alone|Candidate~Editable staging datastore~Not every server supports it|Confirmed commit~Commit requiring confirmation~Support and timeout behavior must be checked',
'Exchange hello capabilities and identify the available datastores.|Lock and edit only according to supported operations; validate the candidate where available.|Commit with an appropriate recovery strategy and verify running and operational state.',
'A lab server advertises candidate and confirmed-commit capabilities.','An automation assumes those capabilities on a server that only offers writable-running.','Discover capabilities first and select a supported workflow; do not issue an unsupported commit sequence.',
'What does get-config retrieve?~Configuration from the selected supported datastore.~It is different from a combined operational data read.|Does an SSH connection prove RPC success?~No; inspect the RPC reply.~A structured error can travel over a healthy session.|Why use a lock?~To coordinate concurrent configuration changes.~It does not guarantee application correctness.|Can confirmed commit replace verification?~No; it provides a recovery mechanism.~Verify the intended state before confirming.', 'netconf','change')
add('s9-g1-t6','RESTCONF maps YANG-modeled data and operations onto HTTP resources using defined media types and protocol semantics.',
'YANG resource~Modeled data addressed through RESTCONF~Not an arbitrary JSON endpoint|Media type~Encoding contract for modeled data~Ordinary JSON labels may be insufficient|HTTP status~Protocol result~Model errors still require interpretation',
'Discover the RESTCONF root and supported model modules.|Address the correct keyed resource with the required encoding.|Use the documented operation and evaluate status, errors and a follow-up read.',
'A client requests a keyed interface using the server’s supported module.','The client assumes a guessed path and ignores an error body because TLS succeeded.','Use the discovered root, exact module and key encoding; preserve sanitized error detail and verify the resource.',
'How differs RESTCONF from generic REST?~It has standardized YANG-related resource and encoding rules.~JSON alone does not provide those rules.|Does RESTCONF guarantee candidate transactions?~No.~Do not copy NETCONF capability assumptions.|Why escape path keys?~Keys can contain characters with URL meaning.~Incorrect encoding selects a different or missing resource.|What should a write test include?~Post-read plus an intentionally denied identity or field.~Verify effect and policy together.', 'restconf yang')
add('s9-g1-t7','YANG describes the structure, types and constraints of management data; it is a modeling language, not the transport used to read or change that data.',
'Leaf~Single typed data value~Its constraints still need validation|List key~Identity of a list entry~Display order is not identity|Constraint~Rule such as range or must~Syntactic JSON can violate it',
'Identify module name, namespace and revision.|Resolve the list keys, leaf types and enabled features.|Validate a candidate instance and compare configuration with operational observations.',
'A keyed interface entry contains an enabled boolean and a bounded MTU.','A client sends a string boolean and an out-of-range MTU.','Inspect the model and reject the instance before sending; use the exact supported types and ranges.',
'Does YANG transmit data?~No; protocols such as NETCONF and RESTCONF carry it.~Model and transport are separate layers.|Why preserve list keys?~They identify the exact entry being managed.~Position-based updates are unreliable.|What does config false mean?~The node is state data rather than configurable data.~Do not try to write it as intent.|Why track revisions?~The available schema can change.~A model name alone is not a complete contract.', 'yang')
add('s9-g1-t8','SNMP automation queries or receives management data identified by OIDs; secure collection requires explicit security level, access views and counter interpretation.',
'OID~Identifier in a management information tree~Numeric identity needs MIB context|Counter~Cumulative measurement~Wraps and resets affect rates|AuthPriv~Authentication and privacy security level~Access authorization is still separate',
'Select an approved read view and authenticated encrypted SNMPv3 profile.|Collect timestamped values and track discontinuities.|Compute rates only over compatible samples and alert on missing evidence.',
'An interface octet counter is sampled twice sixty seconds apart.','A device reboot resets the counter and produces a negative naive rate.','Use discontinuity and uptime evidence; discard the invalid interval and resume with a new baseline.',
'Does an OID value explain its unit?~Not alone; consult its MIB definition.~Bytes and packets are different quantities.|What is the basic rate formula?~Counter delta divided by elapsed time after checking continuity.~A reboot invalidates ordinary subtraction.|Are traps guaranteed delivery evidence?~No; delivery and processing need their own monitoring.~Silence is ambiguous.|Why avoid unrestricted writes?~Monitoring rarely needs them.~Keep collection identity limited to required views.', 'snmp','budget')
add('s9-g1-t9','SSH automation operates over an authenticated encrypted channel, but interactive CLI framing, privilege and command semantics remain application concerns.',
'Host key~Server identity key~Trust on first sight needs an explicit workflow|Channel~Logical stream inside SSH~Prompt completion is not command correctness|Privilege~Allowed command context~Login and enable contexts can differ',
'Verify the host key against an approved baseline.|Use bounded connection and read behavior with a platform-aware command boundary.|Capture sanitized results, inspect device errors and verify the original operation.',
'A lab router returns a prompt after rejecting one command.','The script marks success merely because the prompt reappears.','Parse the error and compare post-state; keep the rejected command visible as a failure.',
'Why reject unknown host keys by default?~To avoid trusting an unverified peer.~Encryption to an attacker is still unsafe.|Why is a shell prompt insufficient?~It signals readiness, not successful intent.~A failed command can return the same prompt.|Should credentials appear in transcripts?~No.~Redact sensitive commands and authentication material.|How handle a read timeout?~Record unknown completion and inspect state before retrying a write.~A slow command may already have executed.', 'ssh paramiko')

add('s9-g2-t0','Netmiko provides platform-aware SSH CLI interactions for network devices; it simplifies sessions without turning textual commands into guaranteed transactions.',
'Device type~Driver selection~Wrong selection can break prompt handling|Command result~Returned CLI text~Error detection remains necessary|Configuration set~Sequence of CLI changes~Partial application is possible',
'Select the driver for the actual supported platform and verify host identity.|Send a bounded read operation and inspect command output.|Apply only a reviewed small change and collect post-state independently.',
'A configuration set contains one valid and one invalid command.','The connection remains open and the first command stays applied after the second fails.','Report partial application, compare the captured baseline and restore only the changed owned setting.',
'Does send_config_set imply atomicity?~No.~Commands can succeed individually before another fails.|Why select device_type accurately?~Drivers handle platform-specific session behavior.~A generic prompt assumption may hang or truncate.|What should a timeout record contain?~Target identity, phase and unknown completion status without secrets.~Do not treat silence as a rejected change.|When is structured API preferable?~When it supplies supported schemas and transaction features needed by the task.~CLI convenience does not add them.', 'netmiko ssh')
add('s9-g2-t1','Paramiko exposes SSH client, transport and channel primitives; callers own command framing, host-key policy and output handling.',
'SSHClient~High-level SSH client object~Explicitly close owned sessions|Known hosts~Approved server keys~Automatic acceptance weakens identity checks|Exit status~Remote process result~Interactive network shells may differ',
'Load approved host keys and keep a rejecting policy for unknown peers.|Connect with bounded timeouts and run a command suited to the remote system.|Read output and errors, inspect completion and close the session.',
'A controlled Linux SSH endpoint writes to stderr and exits unsuccessfully.','The script reads stdout only and claims a successful empty result.','Collect stderr and exit status, preserve failure and close the client even when processing raises.',
'Is AutoAddPolicy peer verification?~It accepts an unknown key rather than confirming an approved identity.~Use a deliberate enrollment path.|Why collect stderr?~Failure detail may be separate from stdout.~Empty stdout is not success.|Can exec_command be assumed on every network device?~No; remote shell behavior varies.~A platform-aware library may be more suitable.|Why explicitly close?~To release network resources predictably.~Garbage collection is not an execution lifecycle policy.', 'paramiko')
add('s9-g2-t2','NAPALM offers a common driver interface for network getters and configuration operations; actual feature support and semantics depend on the driver and device.',
'Getter~Normalized operational read~Coverage varies by driver|Candidate diff~Proposed configuration comparison~A diff is not applied state|Commit~Apply supported candidate~Rollback guarantees are platform-specific',
'Check driver support for the required operation.|Load a merge or replacement candidate into the supported workflow and inspect the diff.|Commit only a reviewed candidate, verify state and use the supported recovery operation if needed.',
'A merge-only change is accidentally prepared as a full replacement.','The diff includes removal of unrelated management configuration.','Discard the candidate; select the intended mode and accept only the bounded diff before any commit.',
'Why distinguish merge from replace?~Replace may remove configuration omitted from the candidate.~Intent must cover the replacement scope.|Does normalized output mean identical device features?~No.~An absent field or unsupported getter needs explicit handling.|What should happen to an unexpected diff?~Stop and discard or revise the candidate.~Do not approve based on its filename.|Can rollback be assumed for every driver?~No; verify support and test it.~A common method name is not a universal guarantee.', 'napalm')
add('s9-g2-t3','Requests is an HTTP client library; a correct automation client still specifies timeouts, validates status and schema, and handles uncertain write outcomes.',
'Timeout~Bound on socket waiting behavior~Not necessarily a total end-to-end deadline|Response~Status headers and body~JSON decoding does not imply success|Session~Reusable client state~Credentials must stay within intended origins',
'Set explicit connection/read timeouts and retain TLS verification.|Check status before interpreting the expected body schema.|Handle pagination and retry only with documented operation semantics.',
'An endpoint returns an HTML error page with status 503.','The client calls json and masks the original HTTP failure.','Check the status and content contract, report sanitized server context and retry only within the read budget.',
'Does requests set a default timeout?~Not for ordinary calls unless supplied.~An omitted timeout can wait indefinitely.|Does r.json prove a 2xx result?~No.~Error responses can contain valid JSON.|What does raise_for_status address?~HTTP error statuses.~It does not validate application fields.|Why not set verify false to fix TLS errors?~It removes server certificate verification.~Repair trust or endpoint configuration instead.', 'requests http')
add('s9-g3-t0','Ansible coordinates declared tasks over an inventory using connection plugins and modules; repeatability depends on module semantics and controlled input.',
'Inventory~Selected managed hosts~Group scope can widen a change|Module~Task implementation~Not all modules are idempotent|Check mode~Prediction when supported~It is not universal execution proof',
'Resolve inventory, variables and connection settings.|Preview supported changes on one owned canary.|Run a bounded batch, verify state and record changed, failed and unreachable outcomes separately.',
'A network task is intended for a single lab group.','The play targets all and changes unrelated devices.','Resolve and inspect the host set before execution, constrain the play and retain a canary gate.',
'Does YAML make a task idempotent?~No; the module and operation determine behavior.~Repeated shell commands may append repeatedly.|Are failed and unreachable identical?~No; failure and inability to reach a host have different causes.~Both prevent complete acceptance.|What does check mode miss?~Unsupported behavior and effects requiring actual execution.~Read the module support notes.|Why pin collections?~Module behavior and dependencies evolve.~Record tested versions with the automation.', 'ansible')
add('s9-g3-t1','Ansible inventory maps host identities, groups and variables to connection targets; reviewing the resolved inventory prevents accidental scope expansion.',
'Alias~Inventory host name~May differ from connection address|Group~Set of hosts~Membership can overlap|Variable precedence~Resolution of competing values~A visible file is not necessarily the final value',
'Load the approved inventory source.|Resolve aliases, groups and effective variables.|Compare the resulting host set with the authorized change scope.',
'edge-1 is an alias whose ansible_host should identify a disposable device.','A group variable points the alias at a production address.','Inspect resolved variables and correct the source; verify that the target list contains only the intended owned hosts.',
'Is inventory_hostname always an IP address?~No; it can be an alias.~Connection variables supply the actual target.|Can a host belong to multiple groups?~Yes.~Overlapping variables need deliberate resolution.|Why inspect effective values?~Precedence can override a nearby declaration.~Reading one file is insufficient.|Should secrets live in an unencrypted inventory?~No.~Use a supported secret mechanism and redact inventory evidence.', 'ansible')
add('s9-g3-t2','A playbook orders plays and tasks over selected hosts, with explicit failure handling and notifications; successful syntax is not successful change execution.',
'Play~Tasks bound to a host set~Host selection is part of the change|Handler~Notified deferred task~Its execution depends on notifications and run behavior|Serial batch~Bounded host subset~Limits blast radius but not shared dependencies',
'Validate syntax and the resolved host list.|Execute a canary with clear preconditions and postconditions.|Expand only after acceptance and keep rollback separate from cleanup.',
'A service update should notify a restart only when its configuration changes.','A task always reports changed and triggers unnecessary restarts.','Use a module or condition that reflects actual change; test a second run and verify no unnecessary restart.',
'Why distinguish cleanup from rollback?~Cleanup removes temporary artifacts; rollback restores prior service state.~Both may be needed after failure.|What is a handler for?~A task triggered by change notification.~It is not proof the service became healthy.|What does serial control?~The number or proportion of hosts processed per batch.~A shared database can still affect everyone.|Why test a second run?~To check repeatability and unintended repeated effects.~The first success cannot establish idempotence.', 'ansible')
add('s9-g3-t3','Cisco Ansible modules live in platform collections with defined resource states and connection requirements; module choice must match the device family and supported release.',
'Collection~Versioned module distribution~IOS and NX-OS are distinct families|Resource state~Declared operation mode~Overridden or replaced can remove existing entries|Connection plugin~Transport integration~Device prerequisites remain necessary',
'Identify the collection, module documentation and platform support.|Inspect the desired resource data and predicted diff where supported.|Run against one owned device and verify resource state and unrelated settings.',
'An interface task intends to merge a description on one port.','A broad replacement removes other interface settings.','Choose the documented merge behavior and explicit target interface; reject diffs outside the intended scope.',
'Can cisco.ios and cisco.nxos be exchanged?~No.~They represent different platform contracts.|Why inspect state semantics?~Some states remove unspecified configuration.~A name such as replaced needs its documented scope.|Is gathered state a deployment?~No; it collects structured data.~Keep observation separate from mutation.|What must a rollback include?~The exact affected settings and their baseline values.~Replaying a generic default is not restoration.', 'ciscoans ansible')
add('s9-g3-t4','Templates turn validated variables into text artifacts; deterministic rendering and explicit missing-variable behavior are prerequisites for reviewable configuration.',
'Template~Text with substitution logic~It does not validate device syntax|Input contract~Required typed variables~Missing data should stop rendering|Rendered artifact~Concrete output for review~Review this output rather than only the template',
'Validate required variables and allowed values.|Render deterministically without contacting a target.|Lint the result and compare a narrow diff before applying.',
'A VLAN template needs an integer ID and a description.','A missing value renders an incomplete command that is still sent.','Fail rendering on missing input and require inspection of the complete artifact before deployment.',
'Does successful rendering prove valid configuration?~No.~A template engine does not implement the target parser.|Why separate render and apply?~To review the exact proposed bytes.~Templates alone conceal resolved values.|How prevent injected newlines in a description?~Validate the allowed input characters and length.~Escaping depends on the target language.|Why deterministic output?~Unrelated ordering changes obscure meaningful diffs.~Stable review reduces accidental approval.', 'jinja')
add('s9-g3-t5','Jinja evaluates templates with variables, filters and control structures; strict undefined handling and a controlled environment prevent missing data from disappearing silently.',
'Environment~Rendering configuration~Set explicit undefined behavior|Filter~Value transformation~It is not automatic domain validation|StrictUndefined~Error on missing variable use~Defaults can still hide missing intent',
'Create a controlled rendering environment.|Provide validated data and reject missing required fields.|Inspect the rendered text and test representative edge cases.',
'A template expects interface.description in every input record.','An absent description becomes empty output under permissive undefined handling.','Use StrictUndefined and make optional fields explicit; test both missing required and intentional empty values.',
'What does StrictUndefined change?~Use of an undefined variable raises an error.~The error belongs before deployment.|Is HTML autoescape suitable for network CLI?~Not as a general CLI quoting rule.~Validate according to the target grammar.|Should templates receive secrets unnecessarily?~No.~Rendered artifacts are often retained for review.|Why test loops with empty input?~An empty collection can remove all generated configuration.~Empty intent needs an explicit meaning.', 'jinja')
add('s9-g4-t0','Git records content snapshots and relationships between commits; the working tree, staging area and committed history are distinct states.',
'Working tree~Editable files~Uncommitted changes are not in a commit|Index~Staged snapshot~A commit uses staged content|Commit~Recorded snapshot and parents~A commit does not deploy infrastructure',
'Inspect working changes and preserve unrelated edits.|Stage only the intended files and review the staged diff.|Create a traceable commit, test that revision and retain the deployment evidence separately.',
'An operator fixes a template but forgets to stage it.','The tested working file differs from the committed version used by CI.','Review staged and unstaged diffs; test the exact committed artifact that will be deployed.',
'Does git commit include every edited file?~No; normally it records the index.~Review staged content.|How differs revert from reset?~Revert records an inverse change; reset moves local references and can alter index or files.~Choose based on history and preservation needs.|Why avoid secrets even in deleted commits?~History may retain them.~Rotate exposed secrets rather than trusting deletion.|Does a clean working tree prove correct code?~No.~It only describes synchronization with the index and commit.', 'git')
add('s9-g4-t1','GitHub and GitLab add collaboration and pipeline controls around Git repositories; branch reviews, runner trust and deployment permissions are separate controls.',
'Review request~Proposed revision for collaboration~Approval must apply to the tested revision|Runner~Environment executing pipeline code~Untrusted changes can execute code|Protected environment~Deployment policy boundary~Repository write access need not imply deployment access',
'Submit a bounded diff with validation evidence.|Run untrusted contributions without privileged deployment credentials.|Approve the tested revision and promote the same artifact under environment controls.',
'A reviewed branch receives a new commit after approval.','A deployment uses an untested artifact built from the changed head.','Revalidate the new revision, refresh required review and bind promotion to the tested artifact digest.',
'Does a green pipeline prove runner trust?~No.~The runner and credential boundaries need separate controls.|Why bind evidence to a revision?~Branches can move.~A status from an older commit may not cover the deployment.|Should fork code receive deployment secrets?~No.~Review code before crossing the privileged boundary.|What differs between repository and environment approval?~One governs code collaboration; the other governs deployment.~The same person or permission need not control both.', 'github gitlab')
add('s9-g4-t2','Version control preserves the history and review context of infrastructure intent, templates and tests; state external to the repository still needs reconciliation.',
'Revision~Identified content state~A branch name is movable|Diff~Change between states~Large unrelated changes weaken review|Provenance~Link from intent to artifact and run~Timestamps alone do not identify content',
'Record intent and its rationale in a bounded revision.|Build reproducible artifacts from that revision.|Attach observed deployment and rollback evidence to the same content identity.',
'A restored configuration is said to be last week’s version.','No commit or artifact digest identifies which bytes were restored.','Record the exact revision and rendered digest; compare post-state with that captured baseline.',
'Why is a commit hash useful?~It identifies a specific recorded content state.~A branch label can later point elsewhere.|Does Git track live device drift automatically?~No.~A collection and comparison process is needed.|What belongs in a change record?~Intent, scope, revision, validation, executor and observed result.~A ticket number alone lacks technical evidence.|Can reverting a template undo all runtime effects?~No.~State migrations or external side effects may require compensating actions.', 'git')
add('s9-g4-t3','Infrastructure as Code expresses infrastructure intent as reviewable machine-readable definitions, then reconciles it with observed resources through controlled execution.',
'Desired state~Approved target definition~Not proof of deployed state|Drift~Difference from approved intent~May be an emergency change requiring review|Plan~Proposed actions~Can become stale before execution',
'Read authoritative intent and current resource state.|Produce and review a bounded plan with destructive actions explicit.|Apply with concurrency control, verify outcomes and reconcile remaining drift.',
'A manually changed firewall object differs from the stored definition.','An unattended apply overwrites the emergency fix.','Classify the drift with its owner, update or restore intent deliberately and regenerate the plan.',
'Is IaC only a script?~No; it includes intent, review and reconciliation practices.~A command list alone may not model state.|Why can a plan become stale?~Other actors or resources can change after observation.~Revalidate before execution.|Should all drift be erased immediately?~No; investigate its cause and authority.~An emergency repair may need incorporation.|Does declarative syntax guarantee safety?~No.~A correct declaration can still request a destructive operation.', 'terraform')
add('s9-g4-t4','Terraform combines configuration, provider operations and state mappings to plan resource changes; state is sensitive operational data and not merely a cache.',
'Provider~Resource API integration~Version affects schema and behavior|State~Mapping from addresses to remote resources~May contain sensitive values|Saved plan~Concrete proposed action artifact~Protect and apply under the intended state context',
'Initialize the approved provider and backend configuration.|Inspect the plan, including replacements and deletions.|Apply with supported locking and verify real resource outcomes and state consistency.',
'Two operators plan against the same resource state.','Concurrent writes produce conflicting state or an obsolete plan.','Use a backend with appropriate locking support, serialize changes and replan after state changes.',
'Does sensitive hide a value from state storage?~Not generally.~Protect the backend and access controls.|What does replacement imply?~A resource may be destroyed and recreated.~Review identity, data and outage effects.|Is every backend lock-capable?~No; behavior is backend-specific.~Verify the selected backend.|Does a successful plan deploy anything?~No.~It proposes actions; apply and post-verification are separate.', 'terraform tfstate')
add('s9-g5-t0','Infrastructure documentation records intent, dependencies and operating procedures with ownership and revision context so another operator can act without guessing.',
'Design record~Decision and tradeoff explanation~A diagram alone omits rationale|Runbook~Executable operational procedure~Requires prerequisites and stop conditions|Evidence~Observed result tied to time and identity~An expected result is not observation',
'Describe service goals and dependency ownership.|Write baseline, failure, recovery and validation procedures.|Exercise the document with another operator and update it from observed gaps.',
'A recovery runbook names a retired management endpoint.','The standby operator cannot reach the control interface during an outage.','Replace the endpoint with verified inventory references and test the independent management route.',
'Why assign an owner and review date?~So drift has an accountable correction path.~Undated prose becomes hard to trust.|What makes a runbook executable?~Specific prerequisites, actions, expected evidence, stop rules and recovery.~A high-level goal is not a procedure.|Should expected output be labelled observed?~No.~Evidence provenance matters during incidents.|What should a decision record preserve?~Alternatives, constraints and the reason for the choice.~Future maintainers need the tradeoff.', 'sre')
add('s9-g5-t1','Network diagrams show selected relationships at an explicit layer; physical links, logical reachability and security policy need distinct labels.',
'Physical view~Devices links and failure domains~A line does not imply independent power|Logical view~Addressing and forwarding relationships~May hide physical dependencies|Policy view~Allowed communication paths~Reachability alone is not authorization',
'Choose the question and layer before drawing.|Label identities, interfaces, zones and shared dependencies.|Cross-check the diagram against inventory and a representative observed path.',
'Two WAN lines appear redundant on a logical diagram.','Both enter the same conduit and fail together.','Add the shared physical dependency and revise the resilience claim or obtain a genuinely separate path.',
'Can one diagram answer every operational question?~Usually not; use explicit views.~Overloaded lines become ambiguous.|Why label line direction?~It distinguishes initiation, forwarding or dependency direction.~An arrow needs a defined meaning.|What demonstrates diversity?~Evidence of separate relevant failure domains.~Different colors are not evidence.|How prevent diagram drift?~Tie objects to stable inventory IDs and review changes.~Screenshots without source ownership age quickly.', 'sre')
add('s9-g5-t2','IP Address Management records address-space allocation, ownership and scope so provisioning can prevent conflicts before activation.',
'Prefix~Allocated address range~Overlap must be interpreted within routing scope|Reservation~Planned assignment~It does not prove active use|VRF scope~Independent routing context~The same address may be valid in different contexts',
'Locate the authoritative prefix and tenant or VRF.|Check overlap and uniqueness under that scope, then reserve the intended address.|Reconcile allocation with observed use and release only confirmed retired assignments.',
'Two provisioning jobs select the same free address in one VRF.','Both read availability before either reservation is recorded.','Use an atomic reservation or serialized allocation and detect the conflict before configuring either target.',
'Is ping silence proof an address is free?~No.~A host may be offline or filter probes.|Can duplicate addresses exist across VRFs?~Yes when routing scopes remain separate.~Global uniqueness is not always the right rule.|Why model reservations?~Intent can precede activation.~A free-looking address may already be promised.|What precedes release?~Confirmation that dependent interfaces, DNS and ownership records are retired.~Deleting a record does not remove runtime use.', 'netbox ip')
add('s9-g5-t3','NetBox models infrastructure intent and inventory relationships, including devices and IPAM; it requires governance and reconciliation with observed infrastructure.',
'Object ID~Stable record identity~A human name may change|Relationship~Modeled association~A valid link can still describe stale reality|Custom field~Local extension~Needs a documented schema and owner',
'Define authoritative fields and ownership rules.|Read records with filters and complete pagination.|Validate relationships and reconcile intended records with independent observations.',
'A device record has the intended management IP but the actual interface differs.','Automation treats the inventory record as proof of live connectivity.','Separate intended and observed values, investigate drift and update only through the designated owner.',
'Is NetBox a packet monitor?~No.~Its intended role is not continuous live forwarding verification.|Why use IDs in updates?~To avoid ambiguity from renames.~Confirm the record still represents the target.|How handle custom fields?~Document type, ownership and validation.~Uncontrolled extensions become competing schemas.|Does an API success prove the network changed?~No; it may only update the record.~Provisioning and observation remain separate.', 'netbox')
add('s9-g5-t4','Configuration management maintains approved settings through baselines, drift detection, controlled changes and verified restoration.',
'Baseline~Approved known configuration~Must be tied to scope and revision|Drift report~Observed difference~Not every difference is unauthorized|Reconciliation~Decision and action to resolve difference~Blind overwrite can remove a valid repair',
'Collect and sanitize current state.|Compare relevant settings with the approved baseline.|Authorize the smallest reconciliation and verify both desired state and protected unrelated settings.',
'A device has an emergency DNS fix not yet reflected in intent.','Nightly automation restores the broken baseline without investigation.','Pause the affected reconciliation, record the emergency rationale and approve an updated baseline or deliberate rollback.',
'Why compare relevant fields?~Volatile counters can create meaningless diffs.~Noise obscures actual configuration drift.|What distinguishes backup from baseline?~Backup is captured state; baseline is approved state.~A backup may preserve a fault.|Why test unrelated settings?~A broad update can have unintended effects.~Success on one field is insufficient.|What closes a drift incident?~An approved decision plus verified resulting state.~A dismissed alert is not reconciliation.', 'ansible terraform')
add('s9-g5-t5','A source of truth assigns authority for specific facts and a controlled update path; it does not imply one system knows every intended and observed value.',
'Authority~Right to define a field~Different fields may have different owners|Observation~Measured current value~Can disagree with approved intent|Reconciliation policy~Rule for resolving differences~Last writer wins is often unsafe',
'Map each field to an authoritative owner.|Collect observations separately with timestamps.|Resolve conflicts through a documented policy and record provenance.',
'Git defines VLAN intent while discovery records actual interfaces.','A sync loop copies observations into intent and then deploys them back, erasing review.','Make synchronization directional per field, preserve provenance and require review for intent changes.',
'Must there be one database for everything?~No; authority can be partitioned by field.~Ambiguous ownership is the problem.|Why avoid bidirectional blind sync?~It can create feedback loops and overwrite intent.~Define conflict rules.|Does latest timestamp imply correctness?~No.~Clock accuracy and authority matter.|What evidence should a conflict retain?~Both values, their sources, times and responsible owners.~Discarding one value hides the cause.', 'netbox')
add('s9-g5-t6','CI/CD links a revision to automated checks and controlled promotion; infrastructure delivery must handle state, credentials and partial changes explicitly.',
'CI~Integration validation~Passing tests have a defined coverage limit|Artifact~Immutable deployable output~Rebuilding can produce different bytes|Promotion~Move tested output toward deployment~Requires environment-specific gates',
'Validate schema, render and test the proposed revision.|Store the exact artifact and review its plan.|Promote to a canary, verify and either expand or restore the captured baseline.',
'A validated configuration artifact is rebuilt during deployment.','A changed dependency produces different bytes from the tested artifact.','Promote the stored digest-identified artifact and fail if its identity differs from the approved one.',
'Why separate build from promotion?~To preserve the tested artifact.~A second build is new evidence scope.|What should a failed canary do?~Stop expansion and invoke the tested recovery path.~Do not continue to meet a schedule.|Does continuous delivery require automatic production deployment?~No.~Approval can remain a deployment gate.|How handle partial application?~Record per-target state and reconcile before retry.~A pipeline exit code alone hides resource state.', 'github gitlab')

add('s9-g6-t0','High availability keeps a service usable through specified component failures using redundancy, detection and controlled failover; shared dependencies bound its protection.',
'Failure domain~Components lost together~Two instances can share one domain|Detection~Decision that a component is unhealthy~False positives can trigger disruption|Failover~Move work to a survivor~Capacity and state must be available',
'Define the protected service and failure set.|Provide independent required dependencies and surviving capacity.|Inject one bounded failure and measure client-visible recovery and integrity.',
'Two service nodes each support 100 requests/s and normal demand is 140.','One node fails and the survivor is overloaded despite redundancy.','Add capacity or reduce demand so the specified failure is supportable; measure service health after the fault.',
'Does two nodes imply N+1 capacity?~No; the survivor must carry required demand.~Count usable capacity after failure.|Can HA replace backups?~No.~Replicated corruption can affect both nodes.|Why use client-visible checks?~A process can be running while the service is unusable.~Measure the real operation.|What weakens independent failure assumptions?~Shared power, storage, network, credentials or software faults.~List the common dependencies.', 'sre','capacity')
add('s9-g6-t1','Disaster recovery restores an accepted service state after a major disruption using protected data, configuration, dependencies and a tested recovery procedure.',
'Recovery point~Usable retained state~A recent copy may be inconsistent|Recovery sequence~Ordered dependency restoration~Applications can depend on identity and DNS|Exercise~Observed restoration test~A written plan is not recovery evidence',
'Define loss and outage objectives for the service.|Retain recoverable data and the configuration needed to use it.|Restore into an isolated target and verify application consistency and access before cutover.',
'Database backups exist but encryption keys are absent at the recovery site.','Restore cannot produce usable application data.','Include protected key recovery and dependency ownership, then repeat an isolated full service restore.',
'Is a backup job success enough?~No; restore and application validation are required.~Unreadable or incomplete copies can still exist.|Why isolate a recovery exercise?~To prevent duplicate identities or writes affecting production.~Recovery targets need controlled connectivity.|What belongs beyond application data?~Configuration, keys, identity, DNS and operational procedures.~Dependencies determine usability.|What should a failed exercise change?~The plan, retention or architecture and a repeated test.~A report without remediation leaves the gap.', 'nist','recovery')
add('s9-g6-t2','Business continuity sustains prioritized business activities through disruption, including people, facilities, suppliers and temporary manual workarounds.',
'Business impact~Consequence of interrupted activity~Technical uptime is only one input|Critical activity~Prioritized business function~Not every system has equal urgency|Workaround~Temporary operating method~Needs staffing, limits and reconciliation',
'Identify activities, owners and tolerable disruption.|Map technical and nontechnical dependencies.|Exercise the workaround and the return to normal, including data reconciliation.',
'A restored order system is inaccessible because staff cannot reach the site.','Technical recovery is reported as full business recovery.','Validate staffed access, communications and the manual-order reconciliation path with the business owner.',
'How differs continuity from DR?~Continuity addresses sustained business activity; DR focuses on restoration of systems and services.~Their scopes overlap but are not identical.|Who sets business priorities?~Accountable business owners with technical input.~Infrastructure staff cannot infer all impacts.|Why test manual processes?~They can fail through missing access or staffing.~A form alone is not a workable process.|Why plan the return to normal?~Temporary records must be reconciled without duplicate or lost work.~Recovery is not complete at system boot.', 'nist','recovery')
add('s9-g6-t3','Recovery Time Objective is the target maximum interruption for a defined service and acceptance criterion; measured recovery time must include the agreed start and finish boundaries.',
'RTO~Target restoration duration~A target is not a measured guarantee|Detection interval~Time before declaring failure~May consume the outage budget|Acceptance time~When required service works~Boot completion may be earlier',
'Define when the outage clock starts and the required service test.|Budget detection, decision, restore and validation time.|Measure an exercise end-to-end and compare with the objective.',
'A 60-minute RTO has 10 minutes detection, 10 decision, 30 restore and 20 validation.','Only the 30-minute restore is reported against the objective.','Count the agreed 70-minute interruption and report a 10-minute gap; reduce the critical path and retest.',
'Is RTO the same as restore-tool runtime?~No.~It covers the agreed service interruption boundary.|What is the serial total in this example?~70 minutes.~Do not omit detection or acceptance.|Can parallel work simply be summed?~No; use the dependency critical path.~The companion model deliberately assumes serial phases.|What establishes compliance with the target?~A measured representative recovery that meets the defined acceptance scope.~One exercise is evidence, not a universal guarantee.', 'nist','recovery')
add('s9-g6-t4','Recovery Point Objective is the target tolerance for loss of recent data, measured relative to an accepted recoverable point and the incident reference time.',
'RPO~Target data-loss interval~Not a promise that every copy is usable|Recovery point age~Time between incident and recoverable state~Schedule alone may understate it|Consistency~Usable related data state~Newest bytes can still be inconsistent',
'Define which transactions and reference time matter.|Verify the most recent consistent recoverable point.|Measure its age and reconcile missing acknowledged work.',
'Backups are scheduled every 15 minutes but the latest successful recoverable point is 40 minutes old.','The team reports a 15-minute RPO as achieved from the schedule alone.','Use the actual usable point age, report the 25-minute gap and repair the failed protection process.',
'Does a 15-minute schedule guarantee 15-minute loss?~No.~Failures and copy lag can age the usable point.|How differs RPO from RTO?~RPO limits data-loss age; RTO targets restoration duration.~Units may match but meanings differ.|Is the newest replica always recoverable?~No.~Logical corruption can replicate too.|What data matters for reconciliation?~Acknowledged transactions after the recovered point.~A filesystem timestamp alone may not express business loss.', 'nist','recovery')
add('s9-g6-t5','Redundant data centers protect against specified site failures only when capacity, data, connectivity and operational dependencies remain usable at the survivor.',
'Site domain~Shared location dependencies~Separate buildings can share utilities|Replication path~Movement of protected state~Latency and bandwidth affect lag|Survivor capacity~Usable resources after loss~Normal distributed load can conceal a deficit',
'Map which dependencies survive a site loss.|Budget replication behavior and remaining service capacity.|Exercise site isolation with fencing, traffic control and application validation.',
'Two sites share one identity service hosted only at site A.','Site B has compute and data but users cannot authenticate after A fails.','Provide a supported identity recovery or redundancy design and include it in the site-loss test.',
'Does geographic separation prove independence?~No.~Carriers, power and control systems can still be shared.|Why plan fencing?~To prevent conflicting writers when communication is lost.~Isolation and failure are not identical.|What constrains synchronous replication?~Round-trip behavior and application write requirements.~Distance is not just a map detail.|What must a site-loss test measure?~Client operations, data consistency and survivor capacity.~A powered-on standby is insufficient.', 'nist sre','capacity')
add('s9-g6-t6','Active/active architecture serves work from multiple instances or sites at once; write coordination, session behavior and failure routing determine correctness.',
'Concurrent service~Multiple active serving locations~Shared mutable state needs coordination|Conflict policy~How competing writes resolve~Silent last-writer rules may lose work|Traffic steering~Selection of serving endpoint~Health and capacity must influence it',
'Partition or coordinate mutable state explicitly.|Route work using health and application constraints.|Remove one active domain and verify both consistency and surviving capacity.',
'Two sites accept updates to the same customer record during a link partition.','Both acknowledge incompatible writes without a conflict policy.','Use a supported consistency and ownership design; test partition outcomes and reconcile according to business semantics.',
'Does active/active eliminate failover?~No; traffic and workload still move after failure.~Remaining capacity matters.|Why are stateless frontends easier?~They reduce local mutable session dependencies.~Their backing data can still be stateful.|Can a load balancer solve write conflicts?~Not by itself.~State coordination belongs in the application or data system.|What must partition testing include?~Which writes are accepted, denied or reconciled.~Availability without correctness is not success.', 'sre','capacity')
add('s9-g6-t7','Active/passive architecture keeps a standby ready to take over from a primary; promotion requires usable state, sufficient capacity and prevention of competing primaries.',
'Primary~Current owner of service work~Ownership needs a reliable rule|Standby~Prepared recovery instance~Readiness can drift while idle|Fencing~Prevent old owner from acting~A missed heartbeat alone is not proof it is stopped',
'Maintain standby state and verify readiness.|Establish that the old owner cannot conflict before promotion.|Promote, validate client operations and plan controlled failback.',
'The primary loses its heartbeat network but continues writing.','The standby promotes without fencing and both write.','Use the supported quorum or fencing mechanism; verify single-writer ownership before admitting traffic.',
'Is heartbeat loss proof of node death?~No.~It can be a network partition.|Why exercise standby regularly?~Dependencies and configuration can drift.~Idle capacity is not verified readiness.|Does failback automatically follow recovery?~No; it needs a controlled plan.~Rushing can cause another outage.|What is promotion acceptance?~Correct ownership plus usable application state and client success.~A role flag alone is insufficient.', 'sre','change')
add('s9-g6-t8','Load balancing distributes requests or connections among eligible backends; health checks, persistence and draining determine how safely membership changes.',
'Health check~Eligibility signal~A shallow check can miss dependency failure|Persistence~Keeping related traffic on a backend~Can skew load or retain a bad target|Drain~Stop new work while existing work finishes~Needs a timeout and connection policy',
'Define a health test representative of the service.|Select among healthy backends with an appropriate algorithm.|Drain a backend for change and verify client errors and remaining load.',
'A backend accepts TCP but cannot query its database.','A port-only check keeps routing failing requests to it.','Use an appropriate application readiness check and validate actual client behavior after removal.',
'Does round robin guarantee equal work?~No; requests have different costs.~Measure workload rather than connection count alone.|How differs liveness from readiness?~Liveness concerns process viability; readiness concerns serving work.~Checks must match their purpose.|Why drain before maintenance?~To reduce interruption to active work.~Immediate removal can break connections.|What follows backend removal?~Check survivor capacity and client error rate.~Healthy membership alone does not establish performance.', 'sre','capacity')
add('s9-g7-t0','Network segmentation defines separate communication scopes and explicit permitted paths; a VLAN or subnet boundary alone is not a complete security policy.',
'Zone~Group with a defined trust purpose~Membership needs identity controls|Policy edge~Allowed source destination and service~Direction and return traffic matter|Enforcement point~Where policy is applied~Uncontrolled alternate paths bypass it',
'Map required service flows and prohibited flows.|Place enforcement on every relevant path.|Test one allowed and one denied flow from the actual source context.',
'Guest clients must reach a public service but never device administration.','Routing between VLANs is enabled without a filtering policy.','Apply explicit least-required policy at the intended boundary and verify guest administration remains denied.',
'Does a different subnet imply isolation?~No; routing may connect it.~Enforcement must implement the policy.|Why test from the real source zone?~The path and applied rules can differ.~An administrator test is not a guest test.|What can bypass a firewall rule?~An alternate path, tunnel or misplaced interface.~Trace the whole route.|What belongs in the policy matrix?~Source, destination, service, direction, owner and justification.~Any-to-any entries hide requirements.', 'planes','policy')
add('s9-g7-t1','The management plane provides administrative access to infrastructure; it needs constrained identities, protected paths and recovery access independent of the service being repaired.',
'Administrative endpoint~Configuration and inspection entry~Exposure widens attack surface|Out-of-band path~Separate recovery access~Independence must be tested|AAA~Authentication authorization accounting~Login success is not command authorization',
'Inventory management endpoints and permitted operators.|Restrict paths and privileges while retaining a tested recovery method.|Audit administrative actions and exercise loss of the normal in-band path.',
'A routing mistake removes the only path to the management interface.','The team cannot reach the device to restore the route.','Use the pretested independent console or management path and restore the captured route without exposing administration to guests.',
'Why separate management from guest access?~To reduce untrusted access to administrative capabilities.~Logical naming alone does not enforce this.|Does out-of-band always mean independent?~No.~It can still share power or authentication dependencies.|What should command accounting record?~Identity, time and action with sensitive values protected.~Audit detail must not leak credentials.|How test least privilege?~Verify permitted reads and denied unauthorized changes.~A successful login is incomplete evidence.', 'planes','policy')
add('s9-g7-t2','The control plane learns and computes forwarding state through protocols and local logic; protecting it preserves convergence and device stability.',
'Adjacency~Protocol relationship~A neighbor does not imply a selected route|RIB~Routing information used for selection~Not identical to installed forwarding entries|Control-plane protection~Limits and policy for control traffic~Overly strict limits can break legitimate protocols',
'Identify required protocol peers and expected state.|Inspect adjacency and route selection before assuming forwarding behavior.|Verify installed state and protect control traffic within measured requirements.',
'OSPF adjacency is healthy but a route loses selection to another source.','Troubleshooting restarts the neighbor instead of examining route preference.','Inspect protocol routes, RIB selection and forwarding installation; change only the actual failing boundary.',
'Is adjacency enough to prove forwarding?~No.~Selection and installation remain separate.|Why protect control-plane CPU?~Excess traffic can disrupt protocol processing.~Policy must preserve legitimate bursts.|Does control traffic always follow the data path?~Not necessarily.~Local termination and handling can differ.|What should a convergence test measure?~Route changes and client-visible loss over time.~A final stable table omits the outage.', 'planes','policy')
add('s9-g7-t3','The data plane forwards or drops traffic using installed state and policy; end-to-end observations must match the packet, direction and actual path.',
'FIB~Installed forwarding information~May differ from control-plane intent|ACL match~Packet-policy decision~Order and context affect the result|Counter~Recorded event count~Aggregate increments may include unrelated traffic',
'Define the source, destination, protocol and packet properties.|Trace ingress policy, lookup and egress handling.|Correlate counters or captures with a controlled positive and negative test.',
'A route is present but an egress policy drops the required flow.','The operator treats a valid route as proof the application should work.','Inspect the actual packet path and policy match; restore the required flow while retaining the denied flow.',
'Does a route prove packet delivery?~No.~Policy, MTU, adjacency and the return path can fail.|Why inspect return traffic?~Many operations require bidirectional exchange.~One-way forwarding is incomplete.|Can aggregate counters identify one test?~Not alone.~Use timing and narrowly scoped observations.|What separates data from management traffic?~The role of the traffic and processing path.~A protocol name alone may not describe every context.', 'planes','policy')
add('s9-g8-t0','Observability is the ability to investigate system behavior from emitted evidence; metrics, logs and traces answer complementary questions with known coverage limits.',
'Signal~Recorded observation~Missing signal is not proof of health|Correlation~Link across observations~Time and identity must align|Hypothesis~Testable explanation~A graph correlation is not causation',
'Start from a user-visible symptom and time window.|Correlate metrics, logs and traces by service and request context.|Test a causal hypothesis and record remaining blind spots.',
'Latency rises after a dependency change while CPU remains normal.','Investigation stops because the infrastructure dashboard is green.','Trace slow requests to the dependency and compare latency and error signals with the change window.',
'How differs monitoring from observability?~Monitoring checks selected known conditions; observability supports investigating behavior from evidence.~The practices overlap.|Why align clocks?~Misordered events can produce a false causal story.~Retain timestamp provenance.|Does no trace mean no failure?~No; sampling or instrumentation may omit it.~Coverage is part of interpretation.|What makes a hypothesis useful?~A predicted observation that could disprove it.~A vague suspicion cannot guide a decisive check.', 'otel sre','budget')
add('s9-g8-t1','Monitoring evaluates selected signals against operational expectations and routes actionable alerts to owners; alert quality depends on user impact and response design.',
'SLI~Measured service behavior~Definition controls what is counted|Threshold~Condition for attention~Static limits can miss context|Alert route~Owner and escalation path~An unowned alert is not an operational control',
'Define a meaningful service signal and evaluation window.|Set actionable conditions and test missing-data behavior.|Exercise notification, diagnosis and recovery before relying on the alert.',
'A probe stops reporting and the dashboard shows its last green value.','The absence of new samples is treated as continued health.','Add freshness detection, mark unknown state explicitly and test the notification path.',
'Why alert on symptoms?~They indicate user impact and urgency.~Internal causes can aid diagnosis without every one paging.|What does stale green mean?~Only that a prior observation was healthy.~Freshness must be visible.|What should an alert contain?~Affected service, time, impact, owner and a usable runbook.~A metric name alone slows response.|Why test the notification route?~A correct rule can still fail to reach an operator.~Delivery is a separate dependency.', 'sre','budget')
add('s9-g8-t2','Logs are timestamped event records that support investigation and audit; structure, correlation, retention and redaction determine their usefulness.',
'Event~Recorded occurrence~A missing event may reflect collection loss|Structured field~Machine-readable attribute~Stable names aid queries|Retention~How long evidence remains available~Access and deletion rules still apply',
'Emit events with severity, identity and sanitized context.|Transport and store them with loss and freshness monitoring.|Query a bounded incident window and correlate with other evidence.',
'An API failure log includes the entire Authorization header.','An operational log becomes a credential exposure.','Redact secrets at emission, revoke the exposed credential and verify that useful correlation fields remain.',
'Why prefer structured fields?~They avoid brittle parsing of free text.~Message wording can evolve independently.|Does debug logging always help?~No; volume and secrets can create new problems.~Use bounded targeted detail.|Why preserve both event and ingestion time?~Transport delay can reorder arrival.~Incident ordering needs the original timestamp context.|What should be tested after redaction?~Both secret absence and diagnostic usefulness.~Removing every field prevents investigation.', 'otel','budget')
add('s9-g8-t3','Metrics summarize measured quantities over time; type, units, aggregation and label cardinality determine whether the resulting calculations are meaningful.',
'Counter~Cumulative quantity~Use reset-aware rates|Gauge~Instantaneous value~Averages can conceal peaks|Histogram~Distribution by buckets~Quantile accuracy depends on bucket design',
'Define units, labels and the measurement population.|Collect with explicit intervals and reset behavior.|Aggregate compatible series and compare distributions as well as averages.',
'A service labels every metric with a unique request ID.','Series count grows with each request and exhausts the monitoring budget.','Keep bounded dimensions on metrics and use logs or traces for per-request identity.',
'Why not average per-host percentiles?~Percentiles are not generally composable that way.~Use compatible distributions for aggregate estimates.|How should counter resets be handled?~By reset-aware rate logic.~Naive subtraction can create negative rates.|What is cardinality?~The number of distinct label combinations producing series.~Unbounded labels can be expensive.|Why state units?~Bytes, bits and durations are not interchangeable.~A correct calculation with wrong units misleads.', 'otel sre','budget')
add('s9-g8-t4','Distributed tracing records spans of work linked by propagated context, allowing a request’s path and dependency timing to be investigated.',
'Trace~Related spans for one execution~Sampling may omit requests|Span~Timed unit of work~Timing does not automatically establish causation|Context propagation~Carry trace identity across boundaries~Missing propagation breaks the chain',
'Create spans around meaningful operations.|Propagate context through supported service boundaries.|Inspect slow and failed traces while accounting for sampling and clock limits.',
'An inbound request calls a database through an uninstrumented client.','The trace shows unexplained parent time without a dependency span.','Instrument the boundary and propagate context; verify the resulting relationship with a controlled request.',
'Does a trace contain every request?~Not necessarily.~Sampling policy determines coverage.|Can trace IDs be metric labels?~Usually avoid this unbounded dimension.~Use dedicated trace correlation.|Why distinguish parent time from child sums?~Work can overlap or remain uninstrumented.~Simple subtraction can mislead.|What should sampling preserve?~The evidence required for the investigation within resource limits.~Document blind spots rather than assuming completeness.', 'otel','budget')
add('s9-g8-t5','Capacity planning forecasts required resources under growth and failure scenarios using measured workload behavior, headroom and provisioning lead time.',
'Demand~Required service work~Average utilization can hide bursts|Headroom~Reserved usable capacity~It must be quantified for the chosen constraint|Lead time~Time to add usable capacity~Procurement completion is not service readiness',
'Measure the bottleneck resource and representative peaks.|Project demand under an explicit growth assumption and failure state.|Trigger expansion early enough to test capacity before the limit is reached.',
'A cluster uses 70 units now, grows 5 units per month and has 100 usable units after one failure.','A six-month procurement starts only when utilization reaches the limit.','Recognize the six-month linear runway and start earlier with a margin for validation and forecast uncertainty.',
'Is CPU the only capacity constraint?~No; memory, IOPS, bandwidth, licenses or staff can constrain service.~Measure the limiting resource.|What is the linear runway here?~Six months before 100 units at five additional units per month.~This assumes unchanged linear growth.|Why model a failed state?~Normal aggregate capacity can disappear during an outage.~Resilience needs survivor capacity.|Does a forecast guarantee demand?~No.~Track error and update assumptions with measurements.', 'sre','capacity')
add('s9-g8-t6','Infrastructure lifecycle management plans acquisition, operation, upgrades and retirement with support, security, dependency and data-handling responsibilities.',
'Support window~Period of available maintenance~Dates and entitlements require verification|Upgrade plan~Controlled transition to a tested version~Compatibility includes dependent systems|Retirement~Removal of service and residual access~Power-off alone leaves records and credentials',
'Inventory versions, owners and dependencies with support evidence.|Test an upgrade and its recovery path before a staged rollout.|Retire data, access, addresses and records under the approved retention policy.',
'An old appliance is powered off but its credentials and DNS remain active.','A replacement or reused address inherits stale access assumptions.','Revoke retired identities, reconcile DNS and IPAM, sanitize data and retain the retirement record.',
'Why verify support dates rather than assume age?~Vendor policy and entitlement vary.~An old or new release is not automatically supported.|What should an upgrade test cover?~Dependencies, data compatibility, service behavior and recovery.~Boot success is too narrow.|Can rollback always downgrade a data format?~No.~Migration compatibility must be tested or a restore path retained.|What completes retirement?~Verified removal of service, residual data, access and authoritative records.~Disposal needs an auditable owner.', 'nist sre','change')
add('lab-9','An architecture rehearsal connects approved intent, generated configuration, policy, capacity and recovery evidence into one reproducible service design.',
'Intent revision~Identified approved design input~It must match the executed artifact|Validation gate~Condition required before change~A passing gate has a specific scope|Recovery record~Evidence of restoration and cleanup~Local file restoration is not enterprise failover',
'Design two sites with management, application and data zones and explicit owners.|Validate intent, render a deterministic plan and rehearse a guarded change in owned local files.|Inject drift and interruption, restore exact bytes and submit the remaining real-platform test plan.',
'A two-site enterprise design uses versioned intent, explicit allowed flows, surviving capacity and a 60-minute RTO with 15-minute RPO.','A stale revision, invalid VLAN or under-capacity survivor is allowed past an unchecked deployment step.','Reject invalid intent and stale revisions, verify a second apply is a no-op, restore the owned baseline and document unexecuted network and recovery claims.',
'Which evidence is actually executed here?~Python validation, local rendering, guarded file changes, hashing and cleanup.~Remote devices and enterprise services are not deployed.|What binds design to execution?~The validated intent revision and artifact digest.~A mutable filename is insufficient.|What must the architecture submission include?~Topology, authority map, policy matrix, failure domains, recovery budget, runbook and observed local evidence.~The original enterprise objective remains a design and guided extension.|What closes an interrupted run?~Restored baseline, verified bytes, removed owned temporary files and an explicit incomplete status.~An interruption must not become an unexplained success.', 'python git nist','change')
